course: dictionary
lesson: 11543
title: Ambulation: walking to get better
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ambulation is a formal word that doctors and nurses use. It means walking or moving about on foot. You will rarely hear it in everyday talk, so the words around it are a big help.
explain: «After her operation, the nurse encouraged early ambulation, so Maya walked to the window and back.» The clues are operation, nurse and walked. Doctors want patients to get up and move after an operation, so ambulation must mean walking about.
explain: «The doctor said ambulation would help Grandpa's legs get stronger after his hip surgery.» The clue is helps get stronger. Try the swap: "walking would help Grandpa's legs get stronger" still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: Ambulation is a noun. It means walking or moving about on foot.
fact: Ambulation is a formal word, mostly used in medicine by doctors and nurses.
fact: «Early ambulation» means getting up and walking soon after an operation, which helps a patient recover.
fact: Ambulation is not the same as ambulance, the vehicle that carries people to hospital. The two words look alike but mean different things.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «After the operation, early ambulation helped Maya get back on her feet.» What does ambulation mean? | Sleeping in bed all day | *Walking or moving about on foot | Riding in a vehicle to hospital | Eating a big meal
exercise: Ambulation and ambulance look alike. Which one is the vehicle that carries people to hospital? | Ambulation | Ambulate | *Ambulance | Balance
exercise: «The nurse asked Sam to try some ambulation after lunch, so he walked slowly down the corridor.» Find and copy the word that means walking about. | corridor | lunch | nurse | *ambulation
test: «Early ambulation after surgery helps the body heal.» Why do doctors want patients to do ambulation? | To make them sleep all day | *To help them recover | To make them feel hungry | To stop them moving for ever
test: Which sentence uses ambulation correctly? | Ambulation is a cake we ate at lunch. | We went ambulation to the shop on the bus. | *The nurse helped Mr Ali with his ambulation along the ward. | He had ambulation in a red car.
exam: Ella says ambulation is the same as ambulance. Why is she wrong? | Ambulance means walking slowly | *Ambulation means walking, and an ambulance is a vehicle | Ambulation is a vehicle that carries patients | They mean exactly the same thing
